## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design a stable multi-key sort and apply bit-operations in a practical setting.

## Key Concepts

- Stable sort
- Two-key comparator
- Hamming weight (popcount)
- Time/space trade-offs

## Code Example

```javascript
function sortByWeightAndValue(arr) {
  const popcount = (n) => {
    n = n >>> 0;
    n = n - ((n >>> 1) & 0x55555555);
    n = (n & 0x33333333) + ((n >>> 2) & 0x33333333);
    n = (n + (n >>> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f;
    return (n * 0x01010101) >>> 24;
  };
  const items = arr.map(v => ({ val: v, w: popcount(v) }));
  const mergeSort = (a) => {
    if (a.length <= 1) return a.slice();
    const m = Math.floor(a.length / 2);
    const L = mergeSort(a.slice(0, m));
    const R = mergeSort(a.slice(m));
    const res = [];
    let i = 0, j = 0;
    while (i < L.length && j < R.length) {
      if (L[i].w !== R[j].w) res.push(L[i].w < R[j].w ? L[i++] : R[j++]);
      else res.push(L[i].val <= R[j].val ? L[i++] : R[j++]);
    }
    while (i < L.length) res.push(L[i++]);
    while (j < R.length) res.push(R[j++]);
    return res;
  };
  const sorted = mergeSort(items);
  return sorted.map(o => o.val);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses understanding of stability in sorting and practical merge logic, not just raw sorting.

## Key Concepts

- Stability guarantees in mergesort
- Two-pointer merge and preserving order on ties
- Time complexity O(n log n) and space trade-offs

## Code Example

```javascript
function stableSortEventsByTs(arr){
  if (arr.length < 2) return arr;
  const mid = Math.floor(arr.length/2);
  const left = stableSortEventsByTs(arr.slice(0, mid));
  const right = stableSortEventsByTs(arr.slice(mid));
  const merged = [];
  let i=0, j=0;
  while (i<left.length && j<right.length){
    if (left[i].ts <= right[j].ts){
      merged.push(left[i++]);
    } else {
      merged.push(right[j++]);
    }
  }
  return merged.concat(left.slice(i)).concat(right.slice(j));
}
```

